Swan's MCP tool names (find_symbol, find_referencing_symbols,
get_symbols_overview), their argument names, and the name-path matching rules
follow Serena (https://github.com/oraios/serena) by Oraios AI. Swan contains no
Serena source code or text; its tool descriptions are its own.

Licensing note, checked 2026-10-25: Serena is licensed per component. Its
SolidLSP library is MIT; the Serena application, where the agent tools live,
is GPL-3.0-or-later (https://github.com/oraios/serena/blob/main/LICENSE).
Copying code or tool descriptions from the Serena application into Swan
would bring GPL terms with it, so Swan follows the interface only.

SQLite (public domain, https://sqlite.org/copyright.html) comes through the
goose-sqlite package (https://github.com/shetautnetjer/goose-sqlite).

tools/ uses scip-typescript (https://github.com/sourcegraph/scip-typescript,
Apache-2.0) as an installed tool; it is not part of this repository.

Structural search comes through the goose-treesitter package
(https://github.com/shetautnetjer/goose-treesitter), which compiles tree-sitter
and its grammars for TypeScript/TSX, JavaScript, Python, Rust, Java, Kotlin,
C, C++, CUDA, C#, PHP, Lua, GDScript, GLSL and HLSL (MIT) and WGSL (MIT); none of their code is in this repository. The live
language-server client is the goose-lsp package (lsp/, a submodule). Structural search follows ast-grep's
pattern syntax and matching rules (https://ast-grep.github.io, MIT); no
ast-grep code is used. The live adapters drive rust-analyzer, pyright and
typescript-language-server, JetBrains' Kotlin language server, clangd
(Apache-2.0 with LLVM exceptions), lua-language-server (MIT) and
shader-validator's shader-language-server (MIT) as installed tools, fetched
from their makers; pyright reads Blender's Python stubs from
fake-bpy-module (MIT).
